H8328":"root","H4716":"decay","H6525":"flower","H80":"dust","H3988":"to reject","H8451":"instruction","H565":"word","H5006":"to spurn","H2734":"to be incensed","H639":"anger","H5186":"to stretch","H5221":"to smite","H7264":"to tremble","H2022":"mount","H5038":"carcass","H5478":"offal","H2351":"outside","H3605":"all","H2063":"this","H7725":"return","H5375":"raise","H5251":"Banner","H1471":"nation","H7350":"distant","H8319":"to whistle","H7097":"end","H4120":"haste","H7031":"swift","H5889":"faint","H3782":"to stumble","H5123":"to slumber","H3462":"to sleep","H6605":"to open","H232":"girdle","H2504":"loin","H5423":"to tear","H8288":"thong","H5275":"sandal","H2671":"arrow","H8150":"to sharpen","H7198":"bow","H1869":"to tread","H6541":"hoof","H5483":"swallow","H6862":"narrow","H2803":"design","H1534":"wheel","H5492":"whirlwind","H7581":"roaring","H3833":"lion","H7580":"to roar","H3715":"lion","H5098":"to groan","H270":"to grasp","H2964":"prey","H6403":"to escape","H5337":"to rescue","H3117":"day","H1931":"he/she/it","H5100":"groaning","H3220":"sea","H2821":"to darken","H6183":"cloud"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","My cousin. So the prophet calls Christ, as being of his family and kindred, by descending from the house of David. (Challoner) (Menochius) — Hebrew and Septuagint, “beloved.” Dod may also mean a near relation. (Calmet) — Isaias being of the same tribe, sets before us the lamentations of Christ over Jerusalem, Luke xix. 41. (Worthington) — The Hebrews had canticles of sorrow, as well as of joy. The prophet thus endeavours to impress more deeply on the minds of the people what he had been saying. The master of the vineyard is God himself, ver. 7. (Calmet) — Hill. Literally, in the horn, the son of oil. (Challoner) — The best vines grew among olive and fig trees. (Doubdan 21.) — Septuagint, “in a horn, (mountain) in a fat soil.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Stones. They burn and starve in different seasons, Colossians xii. 3. — Choicest. Hebrew sorek. (Haydock) — There was a famous valley of this name, Judges xvi. 4. The angels guarded the vineyard, in which Abraham, Moses, &c., were found. — Tower. To keep the wine, &c., Matthew xxi. 33. It denotes the temple, (Calmet) Scriptures, &c. (Menochius) — Wild. Sour, Deuteronomy xxxii. 32."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Judge. God condescends to have his conduct scrutinized, chap. xli. 1."],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Was it. “Why has it produced wild grapes, while I looked?” &c."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Down. By the Chaldeans, and after the death of Christ. (Calmet) — When God withdraws his aid, man is unable to stand. Yet he falls by his own fault, which God only permits. (Worthington)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","It. During the whole of the captivity, the land might keep its sabbaths, Leviticus xxvi. 34. (Calmet) — The people shall be deprived of saving doctrine. (Menochius)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Israel. This comparison is very common, Psalm lxxix. 9., and Matthew xx. 1. (Calmet) — The preceding parable is explained. (Menochius) — Cry. For vengeance, Jeremias xii. 8., and Genesis iv. 10., and xviii. 20. (Calmet)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Even. Septuagint, “to take from your neighbour: shall,” &c. (Haydock)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Things. Unjust practices. — Inhabitant. What will your avarice avail, (Haydock) since you must abandon all? (Calmet)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Measure. Hebrew, “both.” — Thirty. Hebrew, “a chomer shall yield an epha.”"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","To follow. Hebrew, “for shecar,” (Calmet) palm wine, (Theodoret) or any inebriating liquor. (St. Jerome in chap. xxviii.) Our version is conformable to Aquila and Symmachus. (Haydock) — Numbers vi. 3., and Ecclesiastes x. 16."],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Work. Chastisement, ver. 19., and chap. xxviii. 21. (Calmet) — They are admonished to observe the festivals of the Lord, and not to indulge in riotousness. (Worthington)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Hell. Or the grave, which never says enough, Proverbs xxx. 15. Isaias alludes to what should happen under Nabuchodonosor, as if it were past. (G.[Calmet?])"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Justice. All will be taught to adore him. (Haydock)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Strangers. Ammonites, &c., (Calmet) shall occupy part of the land. (Haydock)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Cart. Fatiguing themselves with iniquity, (Wisdom v. 7.; Calmet) and delaying your conversion. (St. Isidore) (Menochius)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","It. The Jews were often guilty of the like insolence, Jeremias xvii. 15."],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Conceits. Blind guides, Matthew xv. 14."],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Drink. Hebrew, “mix shecar.” People generally mixed wine and water. They also strove who could drink most, and the Greeks had a feast for this purpose, (Calmet) which they styled Choas, for the measure which was to be swallowed down. (Aristophanes, Acharn. act. iv. 4. and 5. ultra) — Cyrus the younger boasted to the Greek ambassadors, that “he could drink and bear more wine than his brother.” (Plut.[Plutarch?] in Artax.)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Justice. Declaring the righteous guilty, ver. 20. (Haydock)"],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Still. After the ruin of Jerusalem, the people were led away. (Calmet) — Grievous sins must be severely punished, as was that of the murderers of Christ. (Worthington)"],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Off. Like a king, leading all his subjects to battle. (Calmet) — Whistle. He alludes to the custom of leading forth bees by music, chap. vii. 18. (St. Cyprian) — Earth. The Chaldeans, (chap. xli. 9., and Jeremias vi. 22.) and not the Romans, as some would suppose. — Swiftly. Like an eagle, Daniel vii. 4., and Jeremias xlviii. 40."],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","Broken. They shall march incessantly, Ezechiel xxvi. 7., and xxx. 11."],["haydock",28,null,"haydock","Hoofs. They were hardened, but not shod. (Xenophon) (Amos vi. 13.)"],["haydock",29,null,"haydock","Lion. Nabuchodonosor is compared to one, ver. 26., and Jeremias iv. 7."],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Mist. Denoting calamity. Hebrew, “ruin.” Septuagint, “indigence.” (Calmet)"],["challoner",1,null,"challoner","My cousin.... So the prophet calls Christ, as being of his family and kindred, by descending from the house of David. Ibid. On a hill, etc.... Literally, in the horn, the son of oil."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]},"fa":[[1,2,0,"The Hexaemeron","Homily 5-The Germination of the Earth",0,379,"… He constantly compares our souls to vines. \"My well beloved,\" says He, \"hath a vineyard in a very fruitful hill,\" and elsewhere, I have \"planted a vineyard and hedged it round about. Evidently He calls human souls His vine, those souls whom He has surrounded with the authority of His precepts and a guard of angels. \"The angel of the Lord encampeth round about them that fear him. And further: He has planted for us, so to say, props, in establishing in His Church apostles, prophets, teachers; and raising our thoughts by the example of the blessed in olden times, He has not allowed them to drag on the earth and be crushed under foot. …"],[5,7,1,"Commentary on the Gospel of Luke",null,1,444,"… For he says: \"For the vineyard of the Lord of Hosts is the man of Judah: a plant new and well-beloved: and I looked that it would bring forth grapes, but it brought forth iniquity, and not righteousness, but a cry. And that Israel was thus punished for its neglect of that fruitfulness which was both fitting for itself and well pleasing to God; having neither obeyed His commands, nor consented to perform them; we learn again from' His words, where He says: \"But now I will shew you what I will do to My vineyard. I will take away its fence, and it shall be wasted: and I will rend its wall, and it shall be trampled under foot. …"],[8,10,1,"Commentary on the Gospel of Luke",null,1,444,"… Will you dwell alone in the earth? For these things have been heard in the ears of the Lord of hosts. For though your houses be many, they shall be a desolation: though they be great and fair, there shall be none to inhabit them. For the ground that ten yoke of oxen till shall produce one pitcher full: and he that sows six artabae shall gather three measures,\" Although therefore houses and fields may be the fruit of the oppression of others, yet these, He says, shall lie waste, without inhabitants, and shall yield no profit whatsoever to those who will act wickedly, because the just wrath of God is poured out upon them. …"],[11,null,2,"13 Ascetic Discourses","Discourse 10 -- On Gluttony",2,523,"Hear also the words of the prophecy, and the \"Woe\" which was proclaimed for gluttons, \"Woe unto those who rise up early in the morning and pursue strong drink, and who tarry long over it in the evening while wine inflameth them. With harps, and stringed instruments, and drums, and tabrets, they drink wine, and the works of God they understand not. And behold, the Spirit also hath taught thee that the man who ministereth unto his lusts is unable to understand the works of God. …"],[11,null,2,"13 Ascetic Discourses","Discourse 11 -- On Abstinence",2,523,"Now this is the first lust which conquered the world, and because of it the first transgression of the law took place. Through it also the Prophet reproached the people, when he proclaimed, \"Woe unto those who rise up early in the morning, and follow quickly thereafter.\""],[18,null,3,"Tractates on the Gospel of John","Tractate 10",3,430,"For indeed every man twists for himself a rope by his sins: \"Woe to them who draw sins as a long rope? Who makes a long rope? He who adds sin to sin. How are sins added to sins? When the sins which have been committed are covered over by other sins. One has committed a theft: that he may not be found out to have committed it, he seeks the astrologer. It were enough to have committed theft: why wilt thou add sin to sin? Behold two sins committed. When thou art forbidden to go to the astrologer, thou revilest the bishop: behold three sins. …"],[20,null,4,"Scorpiace","SCORPIACE 1",4,220,"For, in like manner, they also who oppose martyrdoms, representing salvation to be destruction, transmute sweet into bitter, as well as light into darkness. Thus, by preferring this very wretched life to that most blessed one, they put bitter for sweet, as well as darkness for light."],[26,null,5,"Epistle to the Smyrnaeans","Epistle of Ignatius to the Smyrnaeans",5,108,"I Glorify God, even Jesus Christ, who has given you such wisdom. …"]],"fau":[["basil","Basil the Great",1,"father",null,1,379],["cyril-alexandria","Cyril of Alexandria",1,"father",null,1,444],["philoxenus-of-mabbug","Philoxenus of Mabbug",0,"oriental",null,0],["augustine","Augustine of Hippo",1,"father",null,1,430],["tertullian","Tertullian",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,0],["ignatius-of-antioch","Ignatius of Antioch",0,"father",null,0]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 8 (1895)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf208"],["R. Payne Smith (Oxford, 1859); transcribed and partly modernized by Roger Pearse"],["E. A. Wallis Budge, The Discourses of Philoxenus (1894)"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 7 (1888)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf107"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 3 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf03"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 1 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf01"]]}